## 

### Why define Num_Gears as Categorical Ordinal?

-   If we treat `Num_Gears` as Categorical, then we can create an informative plot.

-   Left plot shows average City Miles per Gallon for each body style and number of gears.

-   The right plot is not useful because `Num_Gears` data are treated as integers.

```{r fig.dim=c(14,6), fig.align='center'}

# clean up formatting

gt_plt1 <- gt_cars_all |>
  ggplot() +
  geom_bar(aes(x=Body_Style, y=City_MPG, fill=Num_Gears), 
           stat="identity", position="dodge") +
  labs(y="City MPG", x="Body Style", fill="Num. Gears", 
       subtitle="Number of Gears is Categorical") +
  theme_classic()

gt_plt2 <- gt_cars_all |>
  ggplot() +
  geom_bar(aes(x=Body_Style, y=City_MPG, fill=Num_GearsD), 
           stat="identity", position="dodge")+
  labs(y="City MPG", x="Body Style", fill="Num. Gears", 
       subtitle="Number of Gears is Quantitative") +
  theme_classic()

grid.arrange(gt_plt1, gt_plt2, ncol=2)
```

